          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do bodies disagree about using a short-acting reliever on its own in asthma?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** Yes, on one point. The retrieved guidance agrees that a reliever alone is no longer appropriate. It disagrees about which reliever to use in mild asthma.\n\n**Where the guidance agrees.** The asthma guideline summary (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy) says that treating asthma with a short-acting bronchodilator alone \"is no longer appropriate at any severity.\" It gives two reasons: reliever-only treatment leaves the underlying inflammation untreated, and it is linked to severe attacks even when symptoms are infrequent. It also says that using several reliever inhalers a year is a risk marker in itself.\n\n**Where the guidance disagrees.** The same document says the preferred reliever in mild asthma is \"a genuine point of divergence\":\n- Global strategy guidance describes an inhaled corticosteroid combined with formoterol (a fast-acting long-acting bronchodilator) as the preferred reliever at every step.\n- Some national panel guidance says a short-acting reliever used alongside a regular controller remains appropriate in mild disease.\n\nThe second position still includes a regular controller, so it is not reliever-only treatment. The disagreement is about which reliever to use, not whether a controller is needed.\n\n**Limits of what I could find:**\n- The retrieved summary does not name the bodies behind either position, so I cannot tell you which organisation holds which view.\n- It does not describe how the position has changed over time, so I cannot say whether guidance has shifted.\n- It does not grade how strong the evidence behind each position is.\n- The material is an educational summary, not the full guideline text.\n\n**Safety note:** If an attack does not respond to reliever treatment, or comes back within a few hours, seek prompt medical help. Get emergency help straight away for difficulty speaking in full sentences, breathlessness at rest, confusion or drowsiness, blue lips or fingers, or a chest that has gone quiet during an attack.\n\nQuestions about your own inhalers should go to the clinician who manages your asthma.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
